#!/usr/bin/env python3
r"""warn-install-telemetry-selftest -- prove the install telemetry reminder fires when a Bash
command installs a new tool, and ONLY then.

Sibling of warn-zsh-word-split-selftest, same harness: the hook is run by its shebang
interpreter (what settings.json runs), from a temp directory, with stdin as the payload.
The hook reads the command with conv-shscan.awk (CONV_MODE=install) from its own folder,
so a --hook copy needs the scanner beside it.

WHY THE HOOK EXISTS (#1110, engage-main Q13). Gavin's rule, dropped from every prompt on
3 Oct: an install is done only when the tool's telemetry and update checks are verified
off in the tool's own config; an environment variable is not coverage. This reminder
fires after the install runs (PostToolUse).

    uv run python3 ~/.claude/tools/warn-install-telemetry-selftest
    uv run python3 ~/.claude/tools/warn-install-telemetry-selftest --hook /path/to/copy
    uv run python3 ~/.claude/tools/warn-install-telemetry-selftest --mutants

THE HARNESS PROVES ITSELF FIRST. c01 (`brew install jq`) must warn and c02 (`echo hi`)
must be quiet before any arm counts, so a hook that crashed or printed nothing cannot pass
every quiet arm for the wrong reason.

Fixtures marked "real" are commands taken verbatim from Claude Code transcripts on this
machine (140,693 Bash commands searched on 2026-10-07); a few long ones are trimmed and
say so, and the user name in paths is replaced with `me`.

--mutants proves the arms can FAIL. It writes broken copies of the hook AND of the
scanner (each a plausible bug: reinstall read as install, --help not quiet, option values
read as tool names, the jq shout dropped, ...), runs every arm against each, and fails
unless EVERY arm goes red on at least one mutant and every mutant is caught by at least
one arm. A mutant whose anchor text is missing is a failure too, so the list cannot rot
quietly when the hook changes.

Measured 2026-10-07, branch install-telemetry-hook:

    the hook, /bin/bash 3.2 (its shebang)          88 checks, 0 failures
    the hook, bash 5.3 (copy with that shebang)    88 checks, 0 failures
    --mutants                                       42 mutants, each caught; all 88 arms red
                                                    on at least one

The first --mutants run had four survivors: two arms were missing (an unknown scanner
answer, curl piped into a bare shasum) and two scanner checks did nothing (a -c test and
a --list test that other rules already covered). The arms were added and the dead checks
removed. A replay of all 140,693 Bash commands in this machine's transcripts gave 43
reminders and no BROKEN shout; see the hook's header.
"""

